# Routh II.Art.148 — a couple whose axis is fixed in space — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 148: "A body, at rest,
# free to move about a fixed point O, is acted on by a couple Q whose axis is
# fixed in space. Find the motion."
#
# THE ONE EXACT STATEMENT. Whatever the body does, the angular momentum obeys
#
#     dL/dt = Q        so       L(t) = Q * t
#
# in the FIXED frame, because the couple's axis does not move. The angular
# momentum therefore grows linearly along one straight line in space, for all
# time, exactly. That is true no matter how complicated the rotation becomes,
# and it is the whole content of the article: the hard part of the motion is
# the orientation, not the momentum.
#
# WHY THE MOTION IS NOT SIMPLE. The angular VELOCITY is w = R I^-1 R^T L. If
# the couple's axis happened to lie along a principal axis of the body, then
# w would stay parallel to L and the body would simply spin up about it. Here
# the body is deliberately started TILTED -- rotated 40 degrees about x, so
# that the fixed couple axis (world z) is not a principal direction -- and then
# w is not parallel to L, the body turns, R changes, and w wanders. The body
# tumbles while its angular momentum marches straight.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (0.5, 1, 2) has
# I = diag(5, 4.25, 1.25). It starts AT REST, tilted by the quaternion
#
#     q = (cos 20, sin 20, 0, 0) = (0.9396926207859084, 0.3420201433256687, 0, 0)
#
# (w-first, a 40-degree rotation about x), and carries a constant torque
# (0, 0, 2) applied in the fixed frame. Predicted, exactly:
#
#     L(t) = (0, 0, 2t)      so   L(5) = (0, 0, 10)   and   L(10) = (0, 0, 20)
#
# The cells print L at both instants. The x and y components stay at zero to
# machine precision while the z component tracks 2t; meanwhile |w| climbs and
# the body's orientation becomes complicated.
#
# ENERGY IS NOT CONSERVED HERE, and should not be: a couple does work at the
# rate dE/dt = w . Q. Rising energy in this notebook is physics, not solver
# error.
#
# SETUP NOTE. posim applies obj.torque as a CONSTANT torque in the world frame,
# which is exactly Routh's "couple whose axis is fixed in space". Gravity is
# switched off so nothing else acts.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a box, starting from dead rest at an awkward angle, winding
# itself up into an ever faster and increasingly complicated tumble -- while
# the quantity that actually obeys a simple law, the angular momentum, is
# invisible and perfectly straight.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_fixed_couple
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_fixed_couple.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_fixed_couple.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], orientation = [0.9396926207859084, 0.3420201433256687, 0, 0], torque = [0, 0, 2] }

get obj0.inertia_tensor
get obj0.angular_momentum
get obj0.angular_velocity
energy

run 5 steps 5
# L(5) must be exactly (0, 0, 10)
get obj0.angular_momentum
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
energy

run 5 steps 5
# L(10) must be exactly (0, 0, 20)
get obj0.angular_momentum
norm(obj0.angular_velocity)
energy
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], orientation = [0.9396926207859084, 0.3420201433256687, 0, 0], torque = [0, 0, 2] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
